      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the ability of noninvasive diagnostic tools to predict tubal rupture and active bleeding in patients with tubal pregnancy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective cohort study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Two large teaching hospitals in Amsterdam, The Netherlands."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENT(S)",
          "content": "Consecutively seen patients with suspected tubal pregnancy who were scheduled to undergo confirmative laparoscopy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)",
          "content": "Tubal rupture and/or active bleeding confirmed at laparoscopy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULT(S)",
          "content": "Sixty-five (23%) of 288 patients had tubal rupture and/or active bleeding at laparoscopy. Abdominal pain, rebound tenderness on abdominal examination, fluid in the pouch of Douglas at transvaginal ultrasound examination, and a low serum hemoglobin level were independent predictors of tubal rupture and/or active bleeding. Pregnancy achieved with the use of IVF-ET and the presence of an ectopic gestational sac or an ectopic mass at ultrasound examination reduced the risk of tubal rupture. Abdominal pain was the most sensitive predictor, with a sensitivity of 95%."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION(S)",
          "content": "Because the nonsurgical management of tubal pregnancy should be used only when the risk of tubal rupture and/or active bleeding is low, it can be safely applied in only a limited number of patients."
        }
      ]},
